Start with `overview` for the goals, hard requirements, coordinate system, and
palette. Then read `charge` carefully — the charged node field, the chain-arc
discharge, and critical nodes making the worm dive are the heart of this game.
Then `playfield` (the tile board, the node field, the player band, and the HUD),
`worm` (how the data-worm winds, dives, splits, and grows the field), `foes` (the
glitch, packet-dropper, and corruptor), `controls` (moving the band-bound cursor
and firing up), `flow` (lives, the 12-level run, scoring, and states), and
`assets` (the provided sprite art and how to load it). Finally read `standard`
for the playable mode. The specs cross-reference each
other by name — treat them as one specification. **There is no fixed node field:
it is scattered at the start and grows at runtime, so do not ship a fixed one** —
you design the spawn timing, level pacing, and per-level foe cadence within the
constraints in `worm`, `foes`, and `flow`.

## Build requirements

Deliver a complete, polished, playable game, not a tech demo. It must:

- **Render real graphics** (Canvas 2D, WebGL/WebGPU, or positioned DOM), using the
  provided sprite assets under `assets/` (`{{workspace}}/specs/assets.md`) — not a
  text-only or ASCII rendering.
- **Run in the browser with no backend** — no server, accounts, database, or
  network calls at runtime; everything needed to play is self-contained.
- Require **no API keys or credentials** of any kind to build, run, or play.
- Stay a **Node project** built with only Node.js and npm-installed dependencies —
  no separately installed language toolchain. Extend the provided `package.json`
  at `{{workspace}}` and **commit a `package-lock.json`** (run `npm install` after
  you change dependencies so the lockfile matches) — the build is installed with
  `npm ci`, which requires it. `npm ci` then `npm run build` must produce the
  static site, with no other manual step, into one of `dist/`, `build/`, or `out/`
  at the project root, with an `index.html` at the root of that directory. Serving
  that directory as-is at the root of any static file server must run the game —
  and, because a finished run is played back from a **per-run sub-path**, it must
  also run under **any base path**: load the seeded `assets/` with page-relative
  URLs, never root-absolute ones (`{{workspace}}/specs/assets.md`).
- Include a `README.md` at `{{workspace}}` explaining what the game is, how to
  install dependencies, how to run it in development, how to produce the static
  production build, and the controls.

You choose the language, framework, bundler, and rendering approach within those
constraints — only the `npm ci` / `npm run build` commands and their
`dist`/`build`/`out` output are fixed (plain TypeScript with Canvas 2D is entirely
sufficient). The `reference/` folder under `{{workspace}}` holds screenshots that
are visual targets for the title, gameplay, and game-over screens — match their
layout, palette, and type, but build the screens from the specification rather
than copying the images, and remember the node field, worm, and foes they show are
just one example moment.

## Verify before you finish

Your project has **Playwright** and a headless **Chromium** installed as project
dependencies, plus network access — use them to verify your build, do not just
assume it works. Before you consider the task complete:

- Produce the **static production build** and serve it locally, then open it in
  Chromium with Playwright and confirm it **loads with no console errors** and no
  uncaught exceptions. Also serve it from a **non-root sub-path** and confirm the
  sprite assets still load with no 404s (`{{workspace}}/specs/assets.md`).
- **Drive the game** — move the cursor and fire, wind the worm down the field,
  split it into two worms, let the worm charge a cluster to critical and set off a
  chain-arc discharge, watch a worm dive a critical node, meet a glitch, a dropper,
  and a corruptor, take hits to a loss and (if you can) clear levels toward victory
  — and **screenshot** the title, gameplay, and end screens. Compare each against
  the matching image in `reference/` and against the specs, and fix what does not
  match before finishing.
- **Capture the proof of implementation** exactly as `specs/proof.md` specifies:
  the screenshots and the two short clips (`discharge.mp4`, `worm.mp4`), each
  written to the precise `proof/` path that spec names. These are required
  deliverables of the finished build, not a private check. Do not invent a
  different folder or filename, and do not skip the `.mp4` clips.

Use the project's own Playwright: import it from your project (for example
`import { chromium } from "playwright"`) or run the project-local CLI with `npx
playwright`. There is **no** global Playwright on `PATH` — it lives in your
project's `node_modules`, like any other dependency. Launch Chromium with the
`--no-sandbox` argument — this container has no Chromium sandbox, so a default
launch will fail.

## Commit your work

Your project root is a **git repository** — it was initialized before you started,
with the starter files in its first commit. Your finished implementation is
published directly from this repository's commits, so work you leave uncommitted is
**not** published. When the build is complete and verified, commit everything: run
`git add -A` then `git commit`, and confirm `git status` reports a clean working
tree with nothing uncommitted or untracked. A `.gitignore` already excludes
`node_modules/` and the build output (`dist/`/`build/`/`out/`), so a clean tree
does not mean committing those — but it does mean committing your source, the
provided `assets/`, your `package-lock.json`, your `README.md`, and the `proof/`
artifacts.
